--- CRITERION 2: after the CSVs change, the key is rejected rather than written, at the
    WRITE path after the approval round trip, not only at mint ---
BEHAVIOUR: MET.
Evidence: TestVERIFY_C2b_WritePathRevalidates calls applyResolveTransfer() directly with a
live minted token after rewriting schwab.csv so the pair is no longer suspected. Output:
"C2b OK: write path refused with \"pair_key \\\"f2dd5ca97cb3\\\" is no longer a suspected
transfer awaiting review ...\"", and transfer_decisions.json byte-compared before/after was
unchanged. resolve.go:216-220 is the check that fired.
Black-box note (TestVERIFY_C2a): via the MCP tool surface, the second (token) call is refused
by the PRE-MINT check at resolve.go:95 first, error text "is not a suspected transfer
awaiting review", because the handler re-runs from the top for a token-bearing call. The
write-path check is decisive only when the CSVs change during the blocking approval wait,
which is exactly the window the brief describes. Nothing is written either way.
COVERAGE IN THE DELIVERABLE: **NOT MET — this is the failing criterion.**
Mutation test: in the scratch copy I deleted ONLY the write-path block (resolve.go:208-220,
the "Re-validate against a fresh load before writing" comment through the closing brace) and
left everything else including the new test helper intact, then ran
`go test -count=1 ./internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger/`. Output: "ok
budget2/internal/services/mcpsvc/ledger 0.069s" — all 10 tests still pass. The entire
write-path re-validation, the second half of the fix and the specific behaviour of criterion
2, can be deleted without a single committed test noticing. It has zero regression
protection. No committed test rewrites the CSVs between mint and redeem; I read all ten
functions in resolve_test.go to confirm this.

